# FlashScope — Project Rules

## Time Constraint
This is an 8-HOUR hackathon. Every decision must optimize for speed:
- Don't over-engineer. Ship working code over perfect code.
- If something takes more than 30 minutes and isn't working, use the fallback.
- No tests beyond basic smoke tests. We're not shipping to production.
- Copy-paste is fine. DRY doesn't matter today.

## Integration Contract
- `shared/types.ts` is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the WebSocket API.
- NEVER edit it without telling your teammate.
- Both frontend and backend MUST import types from their local copy of this file.
- If you need a new message type, add it to the WSMessage union and notify your partner.

## Code Style
- Language: English everywhere (code, comments, commits)
- TypeScript strict mode. No `any` types.
- Use the shared type definitions — don't recreate them.
- Prefer simple, readable code over clever abstractions.

## Git Rules
- Dev A works ONLY in `dev/backend` branch (contracts/ + bot/)
- Dev B works ONLY in `dev/frontend` branch (frontend/)
- Commit often with descriptive messages: `feat: price scanner with multicall`
- At H5: merge both into `main` for integration test
- **NEVER add `Co-Authored-By` lines for Claude or any AI assistant in commit messages. All commits must appear as human-authored only.**

## Error Philosophy
- Never crash. Catch errors, log them, continue.
- Backend: if one DEX fails, skip it and scan the others.
- Frontend: if WebSocket dies, fall back to mock data seamlessly.
- Both: prefer degraded functionality over no functionality.

## Demo Priority
The demo pitch is 2-3 minutes. Everything we build must serve the demo:
1. Visual impact (frontend animations, dark mode, data flowing)
2. Live data (real Monad prices showing in real-time)
3. Arbitrage execution (flash loan executing and profit increasing)

If (3) doesn't work, (1) + (2) with replay data is still a strong demo.
